    {
      "question": "e-6237",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "5b86c2619e667d87aabd9abe574be0b6f77acc4d278f006f8dbb22db3294ef5d",
      "new_sha256": "195bef20427632c8d1feeac78cb186948a68a6400d5930674a38eab549ab4a84",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6237",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/assets/c5383ad51988564756ba754cbd6107f487fd0b32be0013f8a89bb78a6900f540.png)",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064318/example-6237.json",
          "file_sha256": "0e1f70ce569c83538320e8bd7385641b897d2dd198a6022210f812ac730c4abc"
        }
      ],
